{
  "case_id": "2010-10-76",
  "document_hash": "abe93e958e2165c1451e147edfaefd3c7f5bdaf65d7003d09cd68da99586ea20",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2010-10-76",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "abe93e958e2165c1451e147edfaefd3c7f5bdaf65d7003d09cd68da99586ea20",
      "evidence_id": "ev-abe93e958e21-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The North Carolina Court of Appeals asserted jurisdiction over Petitioners in this case on claims that arose from a bus accident in France that was unrelated to any North Carolina contacts. Although the Petitioners are located overseas and do not conduct any business in North Carolina, the court held that North Carolina had general jurisdiction over these defendants based solely on the sale in North Carolina of a small fraction of their products.\nUnder this Court's cases, the mere sale of a defendant's products in a State does not permit the State to reach out to assert judicial power over all of that defendant's worldwide conduct."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2010-10-76",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "abe93e958e2165c1451e147edfaefd3c7f5bdaf65d7003d09cd68da99586ea20",
      "evidence_id": "ev-abe93e958e21-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: There's one piece of this I don't quite comprehend. You -- there's no contest that there is jurisdiction over the parent, right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2010-10-76",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "abe93e958e2165c1451e147edfaefd3c7f5bdaf65d7003d09cd68da99586ea20",
      "evidence_id": "ev-abe93e958e21-0000-0004",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 14,
        "start": 14
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the jurisdictional consequences here would be quite disproportionate to the contacts that -- that -- on which it would be based."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. HORWICH",
      "case_id": "2010-10-76",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "abe93e958e2165c1451e147edfaefd3c7f5bdaf65d7003d09cd68da99586ea20",
      "evidence_id": "ev-abe93e958e21-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. HORWICH: No, I don't -- I don't believe\nso, in the sense that I -- I think we were sort of\nassuming arguendo a concept of purposeful availment"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2010-10-76",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "abe93e958e2165c1451e147edfaefd3c7f5bdaf65d7003d09cd68da99586ea20",
      "evidence_id": "ev-abe93e958e21-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: So, why -- why -- I mean, you've heard the argument in the last case. I mean, it\nseemed that potentially can subject the smallest\nmanufacturer to liability throughout the world because\nit uses the Internet. And -- and that -- I don't know\nwhat the foreign policy -- you've heard treaties\ndiscussed, et cetera. Do you want to say anything?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2010-10-76",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "abe93e958e2165c1451e147edfaefd3c7f5bdaf65d7003d09cd68da99586ea20",
      "evidence_id": "ev-abe93e958e21-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Tell me why it is that the\ngeneral principles of jurisdiction do apply here, and\nthen we can get to waiver."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2010-10-76",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "abe93e958e2165c1451e147edfaefd3c7f5bdaf65d7003d09cd68da99586ea20",
      "evidence_id": "ev-abe93e958e21-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: But the \"it\" wasn't the product that caused the injury here. As I understand\nthe case, the tire that allegedly caused this bus to\nturn over was designed for the European and Asian\nmarkets, not the U.S. market."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2010-10-76",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "abe93e958e2165c1451e147edfaefd3c7f5bdaf65d7003d09cd68da99586ea20",
      "evidence_id": "ev-abe93e958e21-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Does -- do any of these\ncompanies, the Goodyear Turkey Company, the others -- do any of them sell the tires directly to\nGoodyear USA for distribution to the United States? As I understand it,\nthese tires were sold to other entities, foreign\nentities, who then sold them to the U.S."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2010-10-76",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "abe93e958e2165c1451e147edfaefd3c7f5bdaf65d7003d09cd68da99586ea20",
      "evidence_id": "ev-abe93e958e21-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Ms. Peddie, this is just a -- this is an I'm-just-curious question: Why do you care? You have Goodyear USA, which has consented to jurisdiction. Why does it make a difference to get these other companies in the North Carolina courts? Does North Carolina not make Goodyear USA substantively liable for this accident?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2010-10-76",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "abe93e958e2165c1451e147edfaefd3c7f5bdaf65d7003d09cd68da99586ea20",
      "evidence_id": "ev-abe93e958e21-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. PEDDIE: Your Honor, North Carolina has particularly Draconian requirements for piercing the corporate veil and alter ego, some of which Petitioners refer to. For example, the proximate causation of the wrong has to be related to the domination and control, and so ideally it would be great if we could go back and simply deal with them and let them collect from their -"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2010-10-76",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "abe93e958e2165c1451e147edfaefd3c7f5bdaf65d7003d09cd68da99586ea20",
      "evidence_id": "ev-abe93e958e21-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Do you have any case law that supports your position, which, I take it -- and correct me if I've got it wrong -- that a subsidiary is subject to jurisdiction wherever the parent is, so long as some products made by the subsidiary are shipped by the parent to the -- to buyers in the forum State?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2010-10-76",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "abe93e958e2165c1451e147edfaefd3c7f5bdaf65d7003d09cd68da99586ea20",
      "evidence_id": "ev-abe93e958e21-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Do you think he'd rather be sued in China?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2010-10-76",
      "confidence": 0.6,
      "document_hash": "abe93e958e2165c1451e147edfaefd3c7f5bdaf65d7003d09cd68da99586ea20",
      "evidence_id": "ev-abe93e958e21-0002-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "uncertain",
      "text": "MS. PEDDIE: I think they would rather be sued in China."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2010-10-76",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "abe93e958e2165c1451e147edfaefd3c7f5bdaf65d7003d09cd68da99586ea20",
      "evidence_id": "ev-abe93e958e21-0002-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: What about special jurisdiction? Why don't -- why we decide this on the basis of special jurisdiction? It's an accepted basis of jurisdiction. Citizenship is. Countries can make it a crime -- in fact, I think Italy does -- to kill an Italian citizen abroad, and that person can be tried for that crime in Italy."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2010-10-76",
      "confidence": 0.0,
